\begin{proposition}\label{PRO3.4}
For any $\gamma \in (1, \frac{8}{3})$, there exists $q \in (1, 4)$ such that
\begin{equation}
     \gamma \le \frac{2q+6}{q+2}.
\end{equation}
    For this fixed $q$, there exists a positive constant $C$, depending on $T$, $q$, $\gamma$, $E_0$, and the initial data $\|\rho_0^{1/(q+2)} v_0\|_{L^{q+2}}$, such that
\begin{equation}\label{L^{q+2}}
\sup_{0 \le t \le T} \|\rho^{\frac{1}{q+2}} v\|_{L^{q+2}} \le C.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
In particular, for any $\gamma\in(1,\tfrac{8}{3})$ there exists such a $q$ for which “$\|\rho^{\frac{1}{q+2}}v\|_{L^{q+2}}$ remains bounded,” which yields the density upper bound via maximal regularity.

\textbf{(2) Density lower bound (key step).} Haspot’s route would require controlling “$\|\rho^{\frac{1}{p+2}}v\|_{L^\infty_T L^{p+2}}$ by a constant independent of $p$,” but here this bound “tends to infinity as $p\to\infty$,” so a different approach is used. The argument leverages the control (from Huang–Meng–Zhang) relating “$\|v\|_{L^\infty}$ and $\sqrt{\log (e^{\frac{25}{9}}+\|\rho^{-1}\|_{L^{\infty}})}$,” but since their method “fails in the whole space when the density exhibits non-zero far-field behavior,” the paper introduces “a novel truncated De Giorgi iteration method.” Concretely, one uses truncations adapted to the far field:
\[
\rho^{-1}_{(k_n)}:=\max\{\rho^{-1}-{k_n},0\},\qquad
U_{n}^{T}:=\| \rho _{(k_n)}^{-1}\| _{L_{T}^{\infty}L^2}^2+\| \nabla \rho _{(k_n)}^{-1}\| _{L_{T}^{2}L^2}^2,
\]
with $k_n:=M(1-2^{-n})+2\|\rho_0^{-1}\|_{L^\infty}$, and $k_0=2\|1/\rho_0\|_{L^\infty}$ “chosen specifically to handle the far-field behavior” while ensuring the initial energy satisfies the convergence condition $U_0^T\le K^{-1/\nu}A^{-1/\nu^2}$. One “choose[s] a suitable $T$ to bound the solution on $[0,T]$,” then uses a “shifted iteration sequence for $[T,2T]$”; if the extension time step is uniform, this yields boundedness up to the maximal lifespan $T^*$, “which in turn establishes the time-dependent lower bound of the density.”

\textbf{(3) Concluding global strong solutions.} With the upper bound (Sections 3–4) and lower bound (Section 4) in hand—along with extending the admissible range of $q$, using Moser iteration to control $\|v\|_{L^\infty}$ by a “$\sqrt{\log V_T}$” term, and then De Giorgi for the lower bound—Sections 5 and 6 “prove the main result by using the established density upper and lower bounds as blow-up criteria.”

