expanded_sketch:
The post-theorem introduction says that "[t]he proof of \begin{theorem}[{see Theorem \ref{theorem l2 del delbar lemma}}]\label{theorem intro l2 del delbar lemma}
    Let $(M,g)$ be a complete K\"ahler manifold such that the self-adjoint Laplacian $\Delta$ has a spectral gap in $L^2\Lambda^{k}_\C$. Given a smooth $L^2$-form $\alpha\in L^2A^k_\C$ which satisfies $\del\alpha=\delbar\alpha=0$, then the following conditions are equivalent:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\alpha=\del\delbar\beta$, for $\beta\in L^2 A^{k-2}_\C$;
        \item $\alpha=\del\gamma$, for $\gamma\in L^2 A^{k-1}_\C$;
        \item $\alpha=\delbar\zeta$, for $\zeta\in L^2 A^{k-1}_\C$;
        \item $\alpha=\del\eta+\delbar\theta$, for $\eta,\theta\in L^2 A^{k-1}_\C$;
        \item $\alpha=d\lambda$, for $\lambda\in L^2 A^{k-1}_\C$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem} relies primarily on new spectral properties of some self-adjoint operators" from \cite{HP} related to the Aeppli and Bott--Chern versions of $L^2$-Hodge theory. More precisely, it outlines the core strategy as follows: assuming $\Delta$ has a spectral gap, one shows that "there are self-adjoint extensions of fourth-order elliptic Aeppli and Bott-Chern Laplacians having a spectral gap, thereby allowing one to use arguments based on elliptic regularity." It then explains why earlier results are insufficient: although \cite[Corollary 8.12]{HP} yields spectral gaps for self-adjoint extensions of the non-elliptic $\Delta_A$ and $\Delta_{BC}$, "[b]eing $\Delta_A$ and $\Delta_{BC}$ non-elliptic, this was not sufficient to prove \begin{theorem}[{see Theorem \ref{theorem l2 del delbar lemma}}]\label{theorem intro l2 del delbar lemma}
    Let $(M,g)$ be a complete K\"ahler manifold such that the self-adjoint Laplacian $\Delta$ has a spectral gap in $L^2\Lambda^{k}_\C$. Given a smooth $L^2$-form $\alpha\in L^2A^k_\C$ which satisfies $\del\alpha=\delbar\alpha=0$, then the following conditions are equivalent:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\alpha=\del\delbar\beta$, for $\beta\in L^2 A^{k-2}_\C$;
        \item $\alpha=\del\gamma$, for $\gamma\in L^2 A^{k-1}_\C$;
        \item $\alpha=\delbar\zeta$, for $\zeta\in L^2 A^{k-1}_\C$;
        \item $\alpha=\del\eta+\delbar\theta$, for $\eta,\theta\in L^2 A^{k-1}_\C$;
        \item $\alpha=d\lambda$, for $\lambda\in L^2 A^{k-1}_\C$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}." Instead, to prove the main theorem, we use the spectral gap of the elliptic Kodaira-Spencer Laplacians, namely:
\begin{theorem}\label{theorem spectral gap kodaira spencer laplacians}
Let $(M,g)$ be a complete K\"ahler manifold. If $\Delta_{\delbar,sw}$ has a spectral gap in $L^2\Lambda^{p,q}$, then for $b\in\{s,w\}$ the operators $\tilde\Delta_{A,sb}$ and $\tilde\Delta_{BC,bw}$ have a spectral gap in $L^2\Lambda^{p,q}$.
\end{theorem}
We also establish a spectral gap for the Varouchas Laplacians:
\begin{theorem}\label{theorem spectral gap varouchas laplacians}
    Let $(M,g)$ be a complete K\"ahler manifold. 
    If $\Delta_{\delbar,sw}$ has a spectral gap in $L^2\Lambda^{p,q}$, $L^2\Lambda^{p-1,q}$ and $L^2\Lambda^{p,q-1}$, then $\square_{A,sw}$ has a spectral gap in $L^2\Lambda^{p,q}$. If $\Delta_{\delbar,sw}$ has a spectral gap in $L^2\Lambda^{p,q}$, $L^2\Lambda^{p+1,q}$ and $L^2\Lambda^{p,q+1}$, then $\square_{BC,sw}$ has a spectral gap in $L^2\Lambda^{p,q}$.
\end{theorem}
Finally, it notes the role of the spectral gap hypothesis: without it, "the images of the closed extensions of $d$, $\partial$, $\delbar$, and $\partial\delbar$ fail to be closed," so the assumption is "essential" for a direct $L^2$ analogue of the classical $\partial\delbar$-Lemma.

