\end{equation}
” and “numerically produce the principal polarization $\theta$, which can be represented as a $2\times 2$ Hermitian matrix (in terms of the map $\mathbf{D}$).”

\item As a check, “the constructed $(A,\theta)$ can be validated by computing its refined Humbert invariant,” using the following proposition.
\begin{prop}
\label{prop: ref_hum_computation}
Let $A = E \times E'$ be a CM product surface over $K$. Let $\theta = \textbf{D}(n, m, kh) \in \mathcal{P}(A)$, where $h$ is a cyclic isogeny in $\Hom(E, E')$. Then we have a basis $\{h, h'\}$ of $\Hom(E, E')$, and let $q_{E, E'}(xh + yh') = [a, b, c]$. We also have
\[
q_{(A, \theta)} = \left[ n^2, \ m^2(mn + 3)a, \ b^2k^2 + 4c, \ -2bm(mn + 1), \ -2bkn, \ 2ka(mn + 2) \right].
\]
