expanded_sketch:
To prove the main theorem, let $\log_{J_Y}\colon J_Y(K_{\mathfrak p}) \to \mathrm H^0(X_{K_{\mathfrak p}},\Omega^1(D))^\vee$ be the logarithm of the $\mathfrak p$-adic Lie group $J_Y(K_{\mathfrak p})$, which we restrict to the direction $W$ of $\Sel(P_0,\Sigma)$. We show that the matrix $M(\Sigma^{\csp})$ represents the dual map $\log_{J_Y}^\sharp \colon \mathrm H^0(X_{K_{\mathfrak p}}, \Omega^1(D)) \to W^\vee\otimes_{\mathbb Q} K_{\mathfrak p}$ with respect to a certain explicit basis of $W$ (constructed using
\begin{equation}
		\label{eq:F-and-Gi}
		F = \sum_{i=1}^r x_i(F) G_i + \div(f)
	\end{equation}
,
\begin{equation}
		\label{eq:f-of-Q}
		(f(Q))_Q = \prod_{i=1}^{k} e_{i}^{a_{i}(F)} \cdot  \left(\prod_{\text{$\lambda$ prime of $\cO_{k(Q)}$}} \pi_{\lambda}^{v_\lambda(f(Q))}\right)_Q \quad \text{in} \quad \left.\left(\prod_Q k(Q)^\times_{\bQ}\right)\right/K_{\bQ}^\times,
	\end{equation}
, and
\begin{equation}
		\label{eq:t-ell}
		\sigma(F) = \sum_{i=1}^s t_{i}(F) u_i \qquad \text{in $V_D$},
	\end{equation}
), consisting of dual bases of the Mordell--Weil group $J(K)$, of the unit groups at the cusps, and of a basis of $\sigma(W)=U$.

For $F\in W$ and $\omega$ a logarithmic differential, the key calculation $\log_{J_Y}^\sharp(\omega)(F)=\int_F \omega$ reduces to calculating $\int_{\mathrm{div}(f)} \omega$ for a certain rational function $f$. A $p$-adic residue theorem (proved later) expresses $\int_{\mathrm{div}(f)} \omega$ in terms of the residues of $\omega$ and the values of $f$ at the cusps. These values can be read off from $\sigma(F)$, which yields the desired conclusion; the main insight is how to combine the $D$-intersection map $\sigma$ with $\mathfrak p$-adic integration.

expanded_theorem:
\label{thm:A}
	Let $\Sigma$ be an $S$-integral reduction type with cuspidal part $\Sigma^{\csp}$ and let $\mathfrak p \not\in S$ be a prime of $K$.
	If $(a_1,\ldots,a_{g+n-1}) \in K_{\mathfrak p}^{g+n-1}$ is contained in the kernel of the matrix $M(\Sigma^{\csp})$ defined in
\begin{equation}
	\label{eq:MSigma}
	M(U) \coloneqq \begin{pmatrix}
		A & B  \\
		0 & C  \\
		0 & D(U) 
	\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
--
\begin{equation}
		\label{eq:matrixD}
		D(U)_{i,j} \coloneqq \sum_{\substack{Q \in |D|,\\ \varphi\colon k(Q) \to \Kpbar}} \varphi(\Res_{Q} \omega_{g+j}) \sum_{\substack{\text{$\fq$ prime,}\\ \text{$\lambda|\fq$ in $\cO_{k(Q)}$}}} u_{i,\lambda} \log \varphi(\pi_{\lambda}).
	\end{equation}
let $\omega = \omega(\Sigma^{\csp}) \coloneqq a_1 \omega_1 + \dots + a_{g+n-1} \omega_{g+n-1}$ and let $c = c(P_0,\Sigma,\omega)$ be the constant defined by
\begin{equation}
	\label{eq:constants}
	c = c(b, \omega) \coloneqq \sum_{\substack{Q \in |D|,\\ \varphi\colon k(Q) \to \Kpbar}} \varphi(\Res_Q \omega) \sum_{\textnormal{$\lambda$ prime of $\cO_{k(Q)}$}} b_{\lambda} \log \varphi(\pi_{\lambda}).
	\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:b-lambda}
b_{\lambda} = -i_{\lambda}(\cP_0,\tilde\cQ) + i_{\lambda}(\Phi_{\fq}(\cpt(\Sigma_{\fq}) - \cpt_{\fq}(P_0)), \tilde\cQ),
\end{equation}
	Then
	\[
		\int_{P_0}^P \omega = c \quad \text{ for all } P \in \mathcal{Y}(\cO_{K,S})_\Sigma.
	\],

