The proof of the main theorem relies on Weinberger’s trick, in which test functions are constructed using eigenfunctions on a round ball to estimate eigenvalues. The stated main difficulties are analyzing the properties of the second eigenfunction of
\begin{align}\label{eq 1.1}
    \begin{cases}
        -\div (e^{h(|x|)}\nabla u)=\l e^{h(|x|)} u, \quad & x\in \Omega,\\
        \frac{\p u}{\p \nu}+\a u=0, \quad & x\in \p \Omega,
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
on the round ball and establishing the required monotonicity to apply Weinberger’s method. The paper’s structure indicates the proof proceeds by first studying properties of the Robin and Steklov eigenvalue problems for balls in the weighted space, and then, in establishing the main theorem, proving the main comparison result and the following corollary.

