sketch:
To obtain finite-time flocking in the sense of \eqref{A-1}, the paper “briefly delineate[s] the following proof strategy in two steps”:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Step A (global classical solutions).} “We first establish a global existence of classical solutions for initial data in $\mathcal{B}=\ell^\infty(\mathbb{R}^d)\times\ell^\infty(\mathbb{R}^d)$ by applying the Schauder-Tychonoff fixed point theory” in $\mathcal{H}=\ell_m^2(\mathbb{R}^d)\times\ell_m^2(\mathbb{R}^d)$. The key compactness input is “the summability $\sum_{i=1}^\infty m_i<\infty$,” which yields that “bounded sets in $\ell^\infty(\mathbb{R}^d)$ are relatively compact in the weighted space $\ell_m^2(\mathbb{R}^d)$,” i.e. a “compact embedding $\mathcal{B}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{H}$.” “Based on this compactness and a suitable a priori bound and continuity of the vector field force,” they “formulate an appropriate solution map and apply fixed-point arguments to obtain a global-in-time classical solutions to \eqref{ICS}.”
\item \textbf{Step B (finite-time alignment / flocking time).} “For the finite-time flocking, the core of the analysis is a \emph{componentwise diameter} framework.” “Instead of relying on energy or radius methods,” they “track the evolution of the velocity diameter in each component and derive differential inequalities in the sense of Dini derivatives.” This “directly captures the finite-time decay induced by the sublinear dissipation and yields explicit upper bounds for the \emph{flocking time},” giving “explicit finite-time alignment criteria and flocking-time estimates,” uniformly for finite/infinite systems, and they also show robustness “under switching sender networks under mild cumulative influence conditions.”
\end{itemize}

