ref_dict.lem: C alpha operator expression:
\begin{lem}\label{lem: C alpha operator expression}
    For a composition $\alpha=(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_{\ell}) \models k$, consider a sequence $\{v_i\}_{1 \leq i\leq k}$ defined by
    \begin{equation*}
        v_i=\begin{cases*}
            \hb_i \quad \text{if } i=\sum_{j=0}^{r} \alpha_{\ell-j} \text{ for some } r \ge 0, \\
            \hhat_i \quad \text{otherwise}.
        \end{cases*}
    \end{equation*}
    In other words, $v_i$ is $\hb_i$ if $i$ is a partial sum of the parts of $\alpha$ starting from the end, and $\hhat_i$ otherwise.
    Let $H(\alpha)=[v_1,v_2,\dots,v_k]$. Then we have
    \begin{equation}\label{eq: C alpha operator}
        C_{\alpha}[-MX^{m,n}] \cdot 1 = \Omega\left(\det([v_1,v_2,\dots,v_k]) \cdot 1\right).
    \end{equation}
\end{lem}

expanded_sketch:
Our proof in establishing the main theorem relies on a fundamental reformulation of the rational shuffle theorem, via a new determinantal approach to the algebraic operators. The approach hinges on defining a family of operators $\mathfrak{h}$, which generate `strictly increasing' cyclic $(m,n)$-parking functions, and then establishing a new formulation of the rational compositional shuffle theorem. We first prove the following lemma. \begin{lem}\label{lem: C alpha operator expression}
    For a composition $\alpha=(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_{\ell}) \models k$, consider a sequence $\{v_i\}_{1 \leq i\leq k}$ defined by
    \begin{equation*}
        v_i=\begin{cases*}
            \hb_i \quad \text{if } i=\sum_{j=0}^{r} \alpha_{\ell-j} \text{ for some } r \ge 0, \\
            \hhat_i \quad \text{otherwise}.
        \end{cases*}
    \end{equation*}
    In other words, $v_i$ is $\hb_i$ if $i$ is a partial sum of the parts of $\alpha$ starting from the end, and $\hhat_i$ otherwise.
    Let $H(\alpha)=[v_1,v_2,\dots,v_k]$. Then we have
    \begin{equation}\label{eq: C alpha operator}
        C_{\alpha}[-MX^{m,n}] \cdot 1 = \Omega\left(\det([v_1,v_2,\dots,v_k]) \cdot 1\right).
    \end{equation}
\end{lem}
This determinantal structure is then used to derive a Jacobi--Trudi identity for the general Schur action $s_\lambda[-MX^{m,n}]\cdot 1$, and in particular to successfully express $e_{(1^k)}[-MX^{m,n}]\cdot 1$ in terms of $k$-tuples of cyclic $(m,n)$-parking functions. The combinatorial heart of the argument (Section~\ref{Sec: sw}) is to establish the necessary relations between the $\mathfrak{h}$-operators, achieved by introducing an $(m,n)$-generalization of the Shareshian--Wachs involution, adapted to cyclic parking functions, which proves the key relations required for the determinantal formulas.

